# Experiment protocol

`run_experiment` accepts a shell `command`, not a prescribed script or seed.
Tell Pi which experiment file to use in chat. It can inspect the source and run
`python3 /project/experiment.py --help` before choosing arguments.

Each command runs via `run.sh <run-dir> <command>` in a fresh `/results/<uuid>`
directory. `$RUN_DIR` is exported for command output paths. For example:

```json
{"command": "python3 /project/experiment.py --help"}
```

Use the discovered CLI for the actual run, pointing outputs to `$RUN_DIR`.
Commands use Bash with `errexit`, `nounset`, and `pipefail`; explicitly handle
expected nonzero statuses when needed. Each call has a 15-minute timeout.
Shell state does not carry between calls; files under `/results` do.

- Read source and datasets from `/project` (read-only Docker bind mount).
- Write logs, checkpoints, plots, and all temporary artifacts under the supplied
  run directory in `/results`. Temporary and cache directories are set there.
- No particular metrics format is required for execution. To use
  `compare_experiments`, write a JSON object of numeric metrics to
  `$RUN_DIR/metrics.json`. Successful commands without metrics (including
  `--help`) remain successful and can be inspected through their logs.
- Use stable metric names and a fixed evaluation budget.
- Check numerical validity and correctness before recording an objective value.
- Run multiple seeds for promising configurations and compare variance.
- When using `uv` for project dependencies, keep `UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT`
  and `UV_CACHE_DIR` pointed under `/results`; read-only project roots cannot
  host an automatically-created `.venv`.
- To customize the runtime, modify `.devcontainer/Dockerfile` in your regular
  development container and rebuild the Pi container.

The bundled `benchmark.py` counts Python source files only as an executable
smoke test. You can run existing project experiments without replacing it or
editing the runner.

Commands have the container user's full permissions, subject to the same
outbound allowlist firewall as Pi. They run as `node` without sudo or firewall
capabilities; only the root entrypoint installs the rules before dropping all
capability bounding bits. External access is IPv4 TCP 443 to startup-resolved
allowlisted hosts, plus DNS to configured IPv4 resolvers; external IPv6 and
other outbound traffic are blocked.

To allow a provider or dataset service, edit
`.devcontainer/firewall-allowlist.txt` on the host or in your regular development
container (one hostname per line, comments accepted), then rebuild the Pi
container to bake the root-owned policy. The agent cannot change it. Restart
for DNS address rotation. See the workspace README for defaults and manual
reachability checks. Image builds are not filtered by this runtime firewall.

The reduced subprocess environment omits model API keys but does not prevent
reading credentials from mounted files or Pi state. Read-only Docker mounts
protect source files, not secrets or services. The firewall is an IP allowlist,
not domain security or total isolation: shared IPs may permit other services,
and permitted services or DNS can carry exfiltrated data.
